  <div class="side-text"><div class="text"><p><strong>NeoPixel Mini Button PCBs</strong> are the smallest ready-to-use NeoPixel format — each on its own tiny board with an input resistor and decoupling capacitor.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<a href="../www.adafruit.com/product/1612">NeoPixel Mini Button PCB</a> — RGB version, sold in packs of 5</li>
<li>
<a href="../www.adafruit.com/product/4776">NeoPixel RGBW Mini Button PCB</a> — RGBW, sold in packs of 10</li>
</ul>
<p>These have no mounting holes or soldering vias…wires must be soldered directly to pads on the back of the PCB.</p>
<p>Each is about 10 millimeters (0.3 inches) in diameter.</p></div></div>
</div>

      <div class="text-element">
  
  
  
  
  
  <h1><div class="anchor-anchor"><a href="./adafruit-neopixel-uberguide__21015941" class="anchor-link"><span class="fa fa-link"></span></a><span id="discrete-neopixel-products-2894456" class="anchor-link-target"></span></div>Discrete NeoPixel Products</h1>
<p>For advanced users needing fully customized designs, <strong>discrete NeoPixel</strong> components are available. You’ll need to provide your own PCB and (depending on the pixel type) surface-mount soldering skill.</p>
<p>It’s <strong>very strongly recommended</strong> that <strong>each NeoPixel have an accompanying 0.1 μF&nbsp;capacitor</strong> between +V and ground. This prevents communication problems due to&nbsp;brownout of the on-pixel driver logic. It’s occasionally&nbsp;sufficient to have <em>one</em> capacitor <em>between pairs</em> of pixels; some of our NeoPixel rings work that way.</p>





</div>

